        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How long does it take to assemble a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As a typical range, a folding home treadmill takes roughly one to three hours for two people following the manual, and a large non-folding machine or incline trainer roughly two to four hours. Walking pads often need little or no assembly. First-time assemblers tend to land at the top of the range; reading the manual first and laying out the hardware by step helps.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does professional treadmill assembly cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Typically somewhere around $100 to $300 in the US for a standard home treadmill, whether through the retailer, the manufacturer or an independent fitness technician. Large machines, upstairs rooms and jobs that include carrying in and removing packaging cost more. These are typical ranges, not quotes, so check with the installer.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can one person assemble a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not safely for most full-size machines. The boxed base unit is a two-person lift, and several steps — standing up the uprights while routing the console cable, holding the console while connecting it — need one person to hold and another to connect and start bolts. Walking pads are the exception, as they often need little assembly.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What tools do I need to assemble a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most treadmills include the hex keys and small spanner needed. A proper set of hex keys, a socket set, scissors or snips for strapping, a spirit level and a torch make the job faster and easier. If you use a cordless driver, set the clutch low and do final tightening by hand to avoid cross-threading.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do you need to lubricate a new treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Follow the manual. Many treadmills ship with the deck already lubricated, some have pre-waxed decks that must never receive silicone, and some manuals ask you to lubricate before first use. Check belt tension and tracking at the same time, and start your lubrication schedule from the first day of use.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it worth paying for treadmill assembly?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, yes — particularly if you have no helper, the machine is going upstairs, it is a heavy non-folding model, or the console is complex. Using the retailer's or manufacturer's service usually puts responsibility for any assembly fault in one place. If you are comfortable with tools and have a helper, doing it yourself is entirely manageable.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

